((date 2026-08-18) (slug entry-ticket-clock26-promotion)
 (hypothesis
  "Does the invented CLOCK half alone (entry_order_max_rest_weeks, with the book-supported re-screen held off) improve outcomes, and is 26 weeks the value to ship? Companion to 2026-08-18-entry-ticket-rescreen, which asked the same question of the other half of the pre-#2349 composite knob and REJECTED it. This entry exists because the clock's default was PROMOTED 0 -> 26 on 2026-08-18 by user decision (PR #2384) outside the normal gate, and the ledger is what the next experiment session reads: after that merge the label 'null' below NO LONGER denotes the shipped default, and every future arm that does not pin entry_order_max_rest_weeks explicitly carries 26.")
 (base_scenario
  "ladder-v4 cell 00 base (top-3000 PIT-2000, 2000-01-01..2026-06-26), split-safe warehouse /tmp/snap_top3000_dedup_v5thin_adj, --no-emit-all-eligible --parallel 1, pinned worktree @59b26c3bf")
 (window_id top3000-2000-2026-single-window-3salt) (baseline_label null)
 (variants
  (((label null-s0) (config_hash rescreen-false-clock-0)
    (aggregate
     (((total_return_pct 281.707836178685) (max_drawdown_pct 39.034688012227853)
       (rejected_fills 262)))))
   ((label null-s1) (config_hash rescreen-false-clock-0)
    (aggregate (((total_return_pct 397.95)))))
   ((label null-s2) (config_hash rescreen-false-clock-0)
    (aggregate (((total_return_pct 265.44)))))
   ((label clock26-draw-a) (config_hash rescreen-false-clock-26)
    (aggregate
     (((total_return_pct 513.42) (max_drawdown_pct 32.46)
       (rejected_fills 206)))))
   ((label clock26-draw-b) (config_hash rescreen-false-clock-26)
    (aggregate
     (((total_return_pct 434.06) (max_drawdown_pct 34.38)
       (rejected_fills 208)))))
   ((label clock26-draw-c) (config_hash rescreen-false-clock-26)
    (aggregate
     (((total_return_pct 377.73) (max_drawdown_pct 34.19)
       (rejected_fills 207)))))))
 (verdict Inconclusive)
 (notes
  "READ THE VERDICT FIELD CAREFULLY. This is NOT an Accept. The verdict type is the closed variant Accept | Reject | Inconclusive (trading/trading/backtest/experiment_ledger/lib/experiment_ledger.mli), so there is no 'Promoted-by-user-override' label to record; Inconclusive is the honest one and it matches the house precedent for a human-gated R3 override, 2026-07-20-bundle-promotion-studies (resistance-v2 BUNDLE, PR #2047), which is also Inconclusive rather than Accept. WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENED: the default was flipped 0 -> 26 anyway, by user decision, in PR #2384. THE CONSEQUENCE FOR FUTURE ARMS, which is the reason this entry exists: 'null' here and in 2026-08-18-entry-ticket-rescreen means (rescreen=false, clock=0), and after #2384 that is no longer the shipped default. Any arm written after 2026-08-18 that omits entry_order_max_rest_weeks silently runs at 26, NOT at the 0 those two entries measured. Pin the field explicitly in every new spec (13 committed specs that arm StopLimit were pinned at 0 in #2384 for exactly this reason). EVIDENCE. Three salts (0/1/2) of a CLOCK-ONLY arm -- re-screen off, so it is a single-knob diff from the null -- returned 513.42 / 434.06 / 377.73 against the null's own three measured draws 265.44 / 281.71 / 397.95 (ladder-v4-seeded-2026-08-14/results.md, salt 0 = 281.71 / salt 1 = 397.95 / salt 2 = 265.44, same base/window/universe/warehouse). Null mean 315.03, spread 132.51pp; clock mean 441.74, spread 135.69pp; MEAN GAP +126.70pp, winning 8 of 9 pairwise comparisons. Exact one-sided rank test: p = 2/20 = 0.100, where 0.050 is the floor achievable at 3-vs-3. Secondary signals move together: maxDD better in all three (32.46 / 34.38 / 34.19 vs the null salt-0's 39.03), rejected_fills lower (206 / 208 / 207 vs 262), realized totals 3.82M / 3.25M / 2.82M vs 2.31M. Mechanism verified: max fill age 26 / 26 / 27 weeks against the null's 865 (27 is expected -- a ticket placed on review week N survives week N+26 and is cancelled at N+27). The salt-to-draw mapping for the clock arm is recorded only in the chain log's stated order and is NOT independently verifiable here; only the SET of three draws is load-bearing, which is why they are labelled draw-a/b/c rather than s0/s1/s2. The null's salt mapping IS verifiable (results.md table above). THE GAP IS BELOW THIS BASE'S OWN NOISE FLOOR. The pre-registered yardstick for this exact base/window/universe is the null's own seed spread: ladder-v4-seeded-2026-08-14/results.md measures it at 132.5pp and states 'no variant gap below it is interpretable'. +126.70pp is BELOW it. (That compares a max-min spread against a difference of means, so it is the rule of thumb this base adopted, not a formal test; the formal test is the p = 0.100 above, and it agrees.) The same file's bar for the only axis that ever cleared the floor was 'all three draws above the null's maximum (397.95)'; the clock's worst draw is 377.73, so it does not clear that either. And nearfloor DID clear it and was still not promoted (memory/project_nearfloor_is_risk_not_return). The distributions TOUCH. THE ARGUMENT THAT DOES NOT DEPEND ON THE BETWEEN-RUN GAP -- and the load-bearing one for the value 26 specifically. A bound is a WITHIN-run cohort claim, and the within-run accounting is unambiguous: on the null arm a 26-week bound cuts 89 fills worth -349,132, the largest net-losing cohort of the four bounds tested (13 / 26 / 52 / 156 cut 147 / 89 / 60 / 25 fills worth -266,866 / -349,132 / -200,906 / -217,518; all four net-losing). Source: dev/experiments/ttl-retest-2026-08-16/README.md clock table, position_id-keyed via ../ticket-funding-cohort-2026-08-18/rest_time_pnl.sh; this is CORROBORATING MECHANISM EVIDENCE, not a significance test and not a bound on the between-run figure. ITS LIMIT: static bucket subtraction ignores capital recycling -- the cash freed by cancelling those 89 fills is redeployed and the table cannot say into what -- so it establishes that the cut population is loss-making, NOT that cutting it is net-positive. THE UNARGUABLE REASON FOR SOME BOUND (defect E): FUL-wein-64 was decided 2000-02-04 and filled 2021-11-01, a resting order that survived 21.7 years. The >3yr population is 20-25 trades, majority losers, net -154,006 / -217,518 depending on the measurement, and upside-free. Unbounded is wrong on its face whatever it earns; that argument selects 'bounded', not '26'. TWO DECLARED DEVIATIONS FROM THE NORMAL GATE, both user-directed and both recorded rather than argued away: (1) promoted WITHOUT the .claude/rules/promotion-confirmation.md confirmation grid; (2) promoted WITHOUT a ledger ACCEPT (.claude/rules/experiment-flag-discipline.md R3) -- this entry is Inconclusive, not Accept. THIRD WEAKNESS: the evidence is NOT reproducible from the repo. The three clock draws live only in chain logs under /tmp; no committed artifact carries them. They are reproducible by re-running dev/experiments/ttl-retest-2026-08-16/specs/ttl-retest-06-clock26-only.sexp (added on main by PR #2377) at TRADING_PATH_SEED_SALT 0/1/2, which determinism makes reliable (the null tripwire reproduces to 15 digits), but that is a weaker record than an artifact. FOLLOW-UP, OWED AND TRACKED: the confirmation grid, queued as VALIDATION OF A SHIPPED DEFAULT rather than as a gate before one -- if it fails, the right response is to reconsider the flip, not to discount the grid. Owner + residuals (>=1 pre-2009 macro cell per promotion-confirmation.md, commit the per-arm outputs, postsubmit watch on goldens-sp500/sp500-2019-2023-armed-stoplimit.sexp which arms StopLimit unpinned and is measured only by golden-runs-sp500-5y.yml post-merge) are recorded in dev/status/simulation.md under '2026-08-18 -- F2 clock default promoted 0 -> 26'. COMPANION ENTRY: 2026-08-18-entry-ticket-rescreen (Reject) covers the other half. The two rules select OPPOSITE populations -- a clock cuts tickets that rested long WITHOUT their setup changing, the re-screen cuts tickets whose stage/sector/macro flipped while resting, which is the base-building pullback-then-resume pattern -- and must never again be conflated behind one knob."))
